{"title":"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 72","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_72&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>On the last afternoon of the Kubik family vacation, Michael puts down a copy of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/a/a4a847745af53834c3d53fc5f9f0baad8c6244bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textit{Mathematical Olympiad Challenges}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"257\" height=\"17\" /> and goes out to play tennis. Wendy notices the book and decides to see if there are a few problems in it that she can solve. She decides to focus her energy on one problem in particular:\n</p><p><i>Given 69 distinct positive integers not exceeding 100, prove that one can choose four of them <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb60e0857a2c0a2c47acfbe81781032feb1f7150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/a/5ba70df5a2f9630194f32f50010cd2e78318c98d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;b&lt;c$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/199b8e3011bb2bfc51393c3ceaffd1af8f18e46c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" />.  Is this statement true for 68 numbers?</i>\n</p><p>After some time working on the problem, Wendy finally feels like she has a grip on the solution. When Michael returns, she explains her solutions to him. \"Well done!\" he tells her. \"Now, see if you can solve this generalization. Consider the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/f/abf8b5ea6e48d604866c2109ff0c816e3c245554.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots,2007,2008\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"221\" height=\"20\" />. Find the smallest value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> such that given any subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/3/9131cc8d7e3501e63cc94162e98596fa66dd7735.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|T|=t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, then there are necessarily distinct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/8/168478aa4d59c78ee7f602e68645b762e4cedfb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c,d\\in T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"94\" height=\"16\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/199b8e3011bb2bfc51393c3ceaffd1af8f18e46c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" />.\" Find the answer to Michael's generalization. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_72&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The statement is FALSE for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/9/ee998b9280496418e47fce8f8c4f978dbc1b88d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=68$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />. The set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c614f1db2f5c6a5c01d449189c94accdfd170c9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{33,34,35,\\ldots,99,100\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"181\" height=\"18\" /> contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/8/9782dd7447f84ce6adcf7945b86038e379c618f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$68$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> positive integers and the sum of any three is at least 33+34+35=102&gt;100. So given any four, the sum of a subset of three exceeds the possible value for the fourth.\n</p><p>The assertion is TRUE for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/3/573c6da44626900ef4370a031471ae3ec5f8f24f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=69$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" />. The proof is as follows: \n</p><p>let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea175781e841662dcf787c5944f70cc49e3e168f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$lo$\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> be the minimum and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/d/39d58a189c50cb2aa3eb374a123d59a79b55c20e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$hi$\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" /> be the maximum and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/e/bbe7986c7cec41922131515707f40156f05d74a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=\\frac{hi-lo}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"89\" height=\"37\" />\nthe set is thus missing the terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/f/17f21dacab75aaed0cce15054411a8f69eaec73f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,\\ldots,lo-1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c61eb838557146e1e23efd9f80e988f253b0dee2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{hi+1,\\ldots,100\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />\nwe have 2 cases:\n</p><p>case 1&#160;:<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/f/e1f4754cd8e6e3a15f1fb0c4817c3b73d4aaa31a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(hi-lo)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> is even\n</p><p>we have the following potential solutions for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/199b8e3011bb2bfc51393c3ceaffd1af8f18e46c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> statement:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/a/31a439cc593e15c23cb94ce1c06e6dc7213a5d32.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1)lo+(z-1)+(z+1)=hi\\]\" width=\"223\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9633133a84664aafcac57fab16ff9f0c5dc3c10.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2)lo+(z-2)+(z+2)=hi\\]\" width=\"224\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/6/08692f6bc793ec9774efb99619f3c043597fd8bf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[...................\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"2\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/d/50db5eb492732acfafa55a1b32b818af2f7039c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q)lo+(z-q)+(z+q)=hi\\]\" width=\"223\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>for the set not to contain an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/199b8e3011bb2bfc51393c3ceaffd1af8f18e46c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> solution, it must be missing either the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/9/c2962ce74dc49485af5f9523c6b7afa91f200367.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z-n$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a00dbeb95012444bd2eaf9de40e5fe467cf3916.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> term (or both) in each of the above equations.\nThe number of equations above is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> which must satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/5/9557a3e4659c7a813442a4352b6e1453b52c8e54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(lo+1)&lt;=z-q&lt;z+q&lt;=(hi-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"302\" height=\"18\" /> . \nThis implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/f/77fd04d6b6cb67efc566176560dab6c113ac7fe2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;=q&lt;=(z-lo-1)=\\frac{(hi-lo)}{2}-lo-1=\\frac{hi}{2}-3\\frac{lo}{2}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"475\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Thus the total number of missing values from the set S is at least the size of the missing beginning terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/9/2192c6f0cbe50a7e2469268a609cd779739f80da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert \\{1,\\ldots,lo-1\\}\\vert$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />\nplus the size of the missing ending terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7c22ccbab67c1a69aa913e8169e8abf6fbdd48a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert\\{hi+1,\\ldots,100\\}\\vert$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"18\" /> plus the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> terms missing in the above equations.\nThis simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/e/e9eb7139cfeee77f46c82afa251bfc432828108a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert missing\\vert&gt;=lo+(hi/2-3lo/2-1)+(100-hi)=(100-1)-(hi+lo)/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"602\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>If the set contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/0/a80952a9a250e7257f7c6d64e763dae189dbb271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$69$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> terms, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/5/c05a2f871cd75683b0761324b8c2adc9d0c58b97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$132&gt;=hi+lo&gt;=70$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"175\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/f/19f9ec4586935c27a6fa8ad2e52fa25b90b7032e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$33&lt;=99-(hi+lo)/2&lt;=64$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"239\" height=\"18\" /> which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/f/b6f3bccae16d5e31266370fced24edc41538d5da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$33$\" width=\"17\" height=\"12\" /> (or more) terms are missing which \ncontradicts the assumption that only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/4/1f4c9c03e9f021b562ff8ab44823db370f193b3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100-69=31$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"109\" height=\"13\" /> terms are missing.\n</p><p><br />\ncase 2&#160;:<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/f/e1f4754cd8e6e3a15f1fb0c4817c3b73d4aaa31a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(hi-lo)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> is odd\nwe have the following potential solutions for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/9/199b8e3011bb2bfc51393c3ceaffd1af8f18e46c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c=d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> statement:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/2/e224ec05799cbcd83d805f63fa7b777d9c7cab1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1)lo+(z-\\frac{1}{2})+(z+\\frac{1}{2})=hi\\]\" width=\"231\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b0567b088d04f7a27ff59b4e582d4fa535ce5f7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2)lo+(z-\\frac{3}{2})+(z+\\frac{3}{2})=hi\\]\" width=\"232\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/6/08692f6bc793ec9774efb99619f3c043597fd8bf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[...................\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"2\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/c/a2cb46ea4dcbfa57eee0909057387f2116410110.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q)lo+(z-\\frac{2q-1}{2})+(z+\\frac{2q-1}{2})=hi\\]\" width=\"312\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>for the set not to contain an a+b+c=d solution, it must be missing either the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7e00a8c912f08b95ad36d7cb825ce5da870f3ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z-\\frac{2n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"85\" height=\"37\" /> or the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/8/878038bd6933691c0f450e92525505b3d18422a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z+\\frac{2n-1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"85\" height=\"37\" /> term (or both) in each of the above equations.\nThe number of equations above is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> which must satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/1/bb1e702e72ef669c51b55ea0ed3f603bd595d5ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(lo+1)&lt;=z-\\frac{2q-1}{2}&lt;z+\\frac{2q-1}{2}&lt;=(hi-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"391\" height=\"37\" /> . \nThis implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d806f013521bcdbb6edd9dc095f4a94f3706dc50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1&lt;=q&lt;=(z-lo-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(hi-lo)}{2}-lo-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{hi}{2}-3\\frac{lo}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"518\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Thus the total number of missing values from the set S is at least the size of the missing beginning terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/9/2192c6f0cbe50a7e2469268a609cd779739f80da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert \\{1,\\ldots,lo-1\\}\\vert$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />\nplus the size of the missing ending terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7c22ccbab67c1a69aa913e8169e8abf6fbdd48a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert\\{hi+1,\\ldots,100\\}\\vert$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"18\" /> plus the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> terms missing in the above equations.\nThis simplifies to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8ece435ddbec69995cbb2e40b6dfa488a489d22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vert missing\\vert &gt;=lo+(hi/2-3lo/2-\\frac{1}{2})+(100-hi)=(100-\\frac{1}{2})-(hi+lo)/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"610\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>If the set contains 68 terms, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b111f91b634802e1fd41159d7e2f6fdad1b0dcfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$133&gt;=hi+lo&gt;=69$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"175\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/e/15efe7e8f708e6c271cba90e0b6d63362f5fe77c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$32.5&lt;=99.5-(hi+lo)/2&lt;=64.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"281\" height=\"18\" /> which is possible if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/6/ab6880d5510ca7fccc639fd3ef870d94b66ee005.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$hi=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59775c8b2458f56bf42678fb8ad321bcbad13c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$lo=33$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p><br />\nFor the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/7/847f97549d7b14ac2b4bbd2bf10ea3e36cea510e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2007$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> we find that the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c55e9ed270e7ca177571541e03ccee557496c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{669,670,\\ldots ,2006,2007\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"200\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/8/b38a7d9c17daec7f294992a3681f70cbc706ac05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2007-669+1=1336$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"177\" height=\"14\" /> elements any three of which sum to at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/1/891d7c57b88e034f9301e2ef2ede10b6a51f1571.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$669+670+671=2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"186\" height=\"14\" />\nBut for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/4/7a437213bd8f525f2508a1bdc801bcda04c5f2b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$668$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> missing terms, we see from the argument above that the assumption of no solutions leads to at least 669 missing terms - a contradiction.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_72&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest\" title=\"2008 iTest\">2008 iTest</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems\">(Problems)</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_71\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 71\">Problem 71</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_73\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 73\">Problem 73</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2008_iTest_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2008 iTest Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=20